## 2.2. Pointers

Pointers are variables that hold the memory address of another variable.

```c
int a = 5;
int *b = &a;

printf("%d\n", *b); // prints 5
```

The asterisk `*` before the variable name indicates that the variable is a pointer. The ampersand `&` before the variable name indicates that the variable is the address of another variable.

## 2.3. Pointers and Arrays

```c
int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int *b = a;

printf("%d\n", *b); // prints 1
printf("%d\n", *(b + 1)); // prints 2
printf("%d\n", *(b + 2)); // prints 3
printf("%d\n", *(b + 3)); // prints 4
printf("%d\n", *(b + 4)); // prints 5
```

